 vibe, political lens, I'm a member of IATSE Local ADC659 and I mostly live in the world where storytelling meets tech. My work floats somewhere between projection mapping, video design, interactive installations, and immersive experiences — often using translucent materials, XR, and other creative tools to mess with perception in playful and poetic ways.\nI’ve been lucky to collaborate with places like the MIT Media Lab, Columbia University, and NYU Abu Dhabi, and my work’s been featured by CBC, The Globe & Mail, and VICE. My first feature doc Finding My Father was nominated for Best Arts & Culture Documentary at the Yorkton Film Festival.\nBeyond making things, I love teaching and sharing what I know. I’ve run workshops on projection mapping and immersive media at the China Academy of Art, Artscape Launchpad, and a bunch of community spaces. Joining ADC659 felt like a natural extension of what I do — being part of a community pushing for more visibility, innovation, and collaboration in the design space of live performance.\nI’m also the co-founder of blcknbrwn, a media arts collective focused on rethinking what diversity actually means — not just race and gender, but viewpoint diversity too. We support work that’s nuanced, bold, and politically engaged.\nMy areas of artistic research dig into the messier parts of the modern world: the military-industrial complex, wealth disparity, the billionaire class, big pharma, identity politics as a distraction, tokenism, censorship, free speech, propaganda, misinformation, and the politics of performance itself. These ideas flow through my work as themes, subtexts, and sometimes just straight-up visual metaphors.\nLet’s make weird, meaningful stuff that questions the status quo — and looks damn good doing it.\n \nAvailable for Work?YesAvailable for Assistant/Associate WorkYes\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\n\n\t\t\t.um-113.um {\n\t\t\tmax-width: 1000px;\n\t\t}\n\t\t\n\n\t\t\t.um-113.um .um-profile-body {\n\t\t\tmax-width